Organize checks into these groups.

A. Crawlability and indexability

- HTTP status codes.
- Internal broken links.
- Broken external links when link checking is enabled.
- Redirect detection.
- Redirect chains and loops.
- Unexpected redirect destinations.
- Robots.txt accessibility and parsing errors.
- Sitemap availability and parsing errors.
- Sitemap URL status and indexability.
- URLs blocked from crawling.
- Noindex directives.
- Conflicts between canonical, sitemap inclusion, and indexability.
- Canonical missing, duplicate, invalid, and inconsistent targets.
- Robots directive conflicts.
- Orphan-page candidates where sitemap and crawl-graph evidence permit detection.
- Crawl depth.
- Unexpected URL parameter variations.
- Duplicate URL discovery.
- Non-HTML response handling.
- Internal links pointing to redirects or error pages.

B. On-page SEO

- Missing, duplicate, empty, short, and long title tags.
- Duplicate, missing, empty, short, and long meta descriptions.
- Multiple H1s and missing H1.
- Heading hierarchy problems.
- Repeated headings.
- Very thin content, with thresholds clearly documented.
- Possible duplicate content using normalized content hashes or similarity methods.
- Missing or incorrect canonical URLs.
- Missing viewport metadata.
- Missing HTML language.
- URL readability, unnecessary parameters, and abnormal URL structure.
- Missing Open Graph metadata.
- Missing Twitter/social metadata where relevant.
- Missing descriptive image alt text.
- Empty anchor text.
- Unhelpful or repeated page titles.
- Internal linking opportunities based on actual crawl data.
- Missing metadata patterns across page templates.

C. Technical SEO and HTML

- HTTPS use and insecure HTTP internal URLs.
- Mixed-content references where they can actually be inspected.
- Missing or malformed response metadata.
- Unexpected MIME types.
- Excessive redirect hops.
- Invalid or contradictory indexing signals.
- Invalid canonical host/protocol targets.
- Invalid hreflang syntax.
- Hreflang target accessibility and return-link problems where data is available.
- Missing or inconsistent x-default when applicable.
- Malformed JSON-LD.
- Multiple or conflicting structured-data blocks.
- Broken structured-data references.
- Basic supported Schema.org type and required-field validation when a real validator is implemented.
- Image loading errors, image metadata, dimensions and oversized assets when their response data is available.
- Pagination and alternate-language signals where applicable.
- Internal/external link attributes.
- HTML language and document structure consistency.
- Actual discovered site-wide technical issues.

D. Performance

- Real per-page response timings.
- Slow responses.
- Transfer sizes.
- Resource-level observations where measurable.
- PageSpeed Insights mobile/desktop test results.
- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP).
- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).
